Master Infrastructure as Code with These Terraform Commands! 🚀✨
Terraform is the go-to tool for managing cloud infrastructure seamlessly through code. Here’s a quick reference of fundamental commands every DevOps engineer should know! 💡⚡

📦 terraform --version – Check Terraform version

🚀 terraform init – Initialize Terraform working directory

🔍 terraform plan – Show execution plan

⚙️ terraform apply -auto-approve – Apply configuration without manual approval

🗑️ terraform destroy -auto-approve – Destroy infrastructure without manual approval

📄 terraform show – Show Terraform state

✅ terraform validate – Validate Terraform configuration

🎨 terraform fmt – Format Terraform code

🔌 terraform providers – List providers used

🗂️ terraform state list – Show managed resources

📋 terraform state show resource_name – Show specific resource details

🗑️ terraform state rm resource_name – Remove resource from state

🔧 terraform taint resource_name – Mark resource for recreation

🧹 terraform untaint resource_name – Remove taint from resource

📥 terraform import resource_type.name id – Import existing infrastructure

♻️ terraform refresh – Refresh state file

🌳 terraform graph – Generate dependency graph

🏠 terraform workspace list – List available workspaces

🔄 terraform workspace select workspace_name – Switch workspace

✨ terraform workspace new workspace_name – Create new workspace

🔄 terraform state mv old_resource new_resource – Rename resource

🖥️ terraform output – Show Terraform outputs

🛠️ terraform apply -target=resource_name – Apply specific resource

💬 terraform console – Open interactive Terraform shell

🐞 terraform debug – Enable debugging logs

📋 terraform plan -var="instance_type=t2.micro" – Pass variable via CLI

🗂️ terraform apply -var-file="vars.tfvars" – Apply using variable file

🧹 terraform workspace delete workspace_name – Delete workspace

🛑 terraform plan -detailed-exitcode – Get exit codes for changes

🔓 terraform force-unlock LOCK_ID – Unlock Terraform state

📤 terraform state push – Manually push state file

📥 terraform state pull – Download current state file

🌐 terraform output -json – Get output in JSON format

🎨 terraform fmt -recursive – Format Terraform code recursively

🗑️ terraform destroy -target=resource_name – Destroy specific resource

🗂️ terraform show -json – Show JSON output

✅ terraform validate -no-color – Validate without color output

Terraform streamlines cloud infrastructure management—keep these commands handy for efficient provisioning and maintenance!
